```apex
public class FollowUpTaskService {

    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // De-duplicate and drop nulls for a clean IN clause.
        Set<Id> uniqueIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id id : opportunityIds) {
            if (id != null) {
                uniqueIds.add(id);
            }
        }
        if (uniqueIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        List<Opportunity> opps = [
            SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :uniqueIds
        ];
        if (opps.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        List<Task> tasksToInsert = new List<Task>();
        List<Id>   taskOppIds    = new List<Id>();
        Date       activityDate  = Date.today().addDays(7);

        for (Opportunity opp : opps) {
            tasksToInsert.add(new Task(
                WhatId       = opp.Id,
                OwnerId      = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject      = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = activityDate,
                Status       = 'Not Started',
                Priority     = 'Normal'
            ));
            taskOppIds.add(opp.Id);
        }

        // Single bulk DML with continueOnError = true:
        //  - Validation-rule rejections are captured in SaveResult (no exception thrown),
        //    so one rejected task does not block the others.
        //  - One DML statement regardless of record count (no 150-statement limit issue).
        try {
            List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(tasksToInsert, true);
            for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
                if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                    failedOpportunityIds.add(taskOppIds[i]);
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Safety net: if the bulk insert throws for an unexpected reason
            // (e.g. a trigger error rather than a validation rule), mark all as failed.
            for (Id oppId : taskOppIds) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(oppId);
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }
}
```

**What I did differently and why:**

I replaced the per-record `insert` in a loop with a single `Database.insert(tasksToInsert, true)`:

| Concern | Per-record loop | Bulk `Database.insert(..., true)` |
|---|---|---|
| 200 records | 200 DML statements → **exceeds the 150 limit** | 1 DML statement |
| Validation-rule rejection on one task | Caught by `try/catch`, others continue | Captured in that record's `SaveResult` (`isSuccess() = false`), others are still inserted — same "rejected task doesn't stop the others" guarantee |
| Returning the failed opportunity IDs | Add to set in `catch` | Check each `SaveResult.isSuccess()`, add the corresponding opportunity Id to the set |

The `true` (continue-on-error) flag is the key: it tells the engine to evaluate every row independently and report per-row results instead of aborting on the first failure. This gives us exactly the semantics the per-record `try/catch` was trying to achieve, but in one governor-safe DML call.

## Root Cause

`PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(Id)` executes one SOQL per call. 200 lines → 200 queries → governor limit (100/tx) exceeded. Since I cannot modify `PricingRules.cls`, I batch the product lookup in my handler (one SOQL) and then delegate the discount determination to `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family)`, which is pure in-memory logic. The rules (Software=15, Services=5, other=0) stay in `PricingRules` — I'm not copying them.

